; exit 71 (see .exit)
; ABORT TO AN ARENA MARK, for the unhandled-effect trap - and for the
; one restore that is not about memory at all.
;
; An effect operation with no handler in dynamic extent exits 71
; (`tests/stdlib/310-effect-unhandled.ax` pins that). Inside a
; recovery point it answers 71 to the arming call instead, and the
; program carries on.
;
; THE EVIDENCE SLOTS ARE THE HARD PART. `handle` pushes an evidence
; record onto `@__axiom_ev_<Effect>` and pops it at the end of its
; body. An abort jumps past every pending pop, and the records those
; pops would have restored live in the arena ABOVE the mark - which
; the abort's reset reclaims. `MM-ALLOC-16b` names the consequence
; exactly: the slot still points at a reclaimed record and the next
; operation dispatches through it. So the arming call snapshots every
; slot and the abort writes them all back.
;
; `underHandler` is what would notice if it did not. It installs a
; handler, arms a recovery point under it, and the extent it protects
; installs a SECOND handler and then divides by zero - so the abort
; jumps out of a live `handle` whose record is above the mark. The
; `log` afterwards must reach the outer handler and answer 7. The two
; numbers are folded into one so the golden cannot pass on half of it:
; `72 * 10 + 7`, the recovered status and the outer handler's answer.
;
; The `handle` in the aborted extent is also the shape that abandons a
; RETAIN on a block below the mark - `emitHandleDyn` takes one on the
; record its push displaced, and the `release` that returns it is at
; the pop this jump skips. That leak is bounded and measured by
; `scripts/check-recover.sh`; it is named here because this is the
; fixture whose shape produces it.
(import IO)

(import Str)

(effect Console
  (log :: (-> String Int)))

(:: zero Int)

(fn (zero) 0)

(:: shout (-> Int Int))

(fn (shout x) (log "nobody home"))

(:: nested (-> Int Int))

(fn (nested x)
  (handle {
    (log "reached the inner handler")
    (/ 1 zero)
  } (Console) (lambda (s) 0))
)

(:: underHandler (-> Int Int))

(fn (underHandler x)
  (handle (let (
    (n (__axiom_recover
      __axiom_arena_mark      (lambda (y) (nested y))
    ))
    (l (log "the outer handler is still installed"))
  )
    (+ (* n 10) l)
  ) (Console Alloc) (lambda (s) 7))
)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (let (
    (inside (__axiom_recover
      __axiom_arena_mark      (lambda (x) (shout x))
    ))
    (after (underHandler 0))
  )
    {
      (println "recovered {inside}")
      (println "slots {after}")
      ; Outside every recovery point: 71, on fd 2, and the program
      ; stops here.
      (shout 0)
      ; Spelled `printlnLit` and not `println` for the reason
      ; `314-out-of-memory.ax` spells its own unreachable line that
      ; way: `verify-stdlib-out.py` MODELS `(println "literal")` as a
      ; claim that the literal appears in the golden, and this line is
      ; deliberately never printed. It is still a tripwire - if the
      ; trap outside a recovery point ever stops stopping the program,
      ; stdout gains a line and the golden says so.
      (printlnLit (strCStr "UNREACHABLE: the trap outside a recovery point returned"))
    }
  )
)
